es, benchmark rates or valuations.\n\n**Debt (n5.r1)**\n- Utility bond spreads widened 30bp in Q3 2026 to 165bp. That makes borrowing costlier, but the market isn't shut.\n- Norvane priced a C$1.1B bond \"comfortably\". Its 2027 capex is up 12%, and one deal doesn't prove the whole programme is covered.\n- Pellham plans 2027 capex up 18%. It is cutting 2027 transformer orders by about 40% and deferring two substation programmes \"until financing improves\".\n- The documents don't say why Pellham didn't close its gap with debt. Leverage or rating limits are possible, but that is inference.\n\n**Equity (n6.r1)**\n- Pellham withdrew a planned C$2.4B equity offering after investor demand fell short.\n- Two other utility share offerings were cut back in size in Q3. That suggests weak appetite for utility equity generally.\n- I found no valuation or share-price data, so I can't quantify dilution. Downsized and withdrawn deals imply any raise now would be priced at a discount.\n\n**Implication:** Expect 2027 capex to lean more on costlier debt and to slip, mainly at Pellham. For grid-equipment demand, use Pellham's scaled-back numbers, not the headline +18%.\n\n**What would change this:**\n- Spreads tightening back toward about 135bp.\n- Pellham returning with a debt or hybrid deal.\n- Pellham restoring the deferred programmes.\n- The equity window reopening.","ttft_ms":1490,"type":"result","duration_ms":28706,"uuid":"825d9cf2-9a66-4c24-a19c-5e3206ab6571","ttft_stream_ms":891,"time_to_request_ms":157,"time_to_request_phases_ms":{"other":10,"system_prompt":10,"resume_other":0,"process_user_input":10,"input_attachments":20,"input_hooks":7,"conversation_append":0,"transcript_persist":16,"tool_pool_refresh":1,"init_message":14,"init_frame_send":2,"engine_pickup":1,"loop_entry":7,"message_prep":0,"model_config_wait":1,"autocompact":5,"request_announcements":19,"query_setup":0,"model_gates":0,"tool_schema_build":9,"message_normalization":2,"client_creation":16,"request_build":7}